# Bitmap SDK

One on-chain JS SDK for:
• Bitmap validation
• Parcel validation + tie-breaks
• Mondrian geometry
• provenance + reinscriptions
• shared IndexedDB caching and reusable block-coverage indexing
• live block watching

One import. No rebuilding the same Bitmap logic in every app.

https://switch-900.github.io/bitmap-onchain-sdk/


**On-chain JavaScript SDK for Bitmap and Parcel development on Bitcoin.**

Bitmap SDK provides a single reusable API for:

- Canonical Bitmap validation
- Bitmap inscription and sat resolution
- Parcel discovery and validation
- Parcel protocol rules and tie-breaks
- Bitmap Mondrian geometry
- Parent/child provenance
- Same-sat reinscription history
- Local IndexedDB indexing
- Bitcoin block scanning
- New Bitmap and Parcel detection
- Block watching and reorg handling

Bitcoin and Ord recursive endpoints remain the source of truth.

IndexedDB is used only as a validated local accelerator.

---

## Import

Bitmap SDK v1 is published on a canonical sat.

```js
const {
  default: BitmapSDK
} = await import(
  '/r/sat/25483050779204/at/-1/content'
)
```

Using the sat path means applications can continue importing the same URL when a compatible SDK update is reinscribed on that sat.

---

## Quick start

For a known Bitmap, or when a complete canonical sync is acceptable, start with:

```js
const {
  default: BitmapSDK
} = await import(
  '/r/sat/25483050779204/at/-1/content'
)

const data =
  await BitmapSDK.getBitmapData(
    177700
  )

console.log(data.bitmap)
console.log(data.parcels)
console.log(data.mondrian)
```

For interactive discovery of a post-OCI or otherwise unknown Bitmap, call
`scanBitmapClaim()` first. `getBitmapData()` performs a complete sync when
its stored state is absent or stale, so it is intentionally not a bounded UI
operation.

---

# Bitmap validation

Validate a Bitmap:

```js
const bitmap =
  await BitmapSDK.validateBitmap(
    177700
  )
```

Get its canonical inscription ID:

```js
const inscriptionId =
  await BitmapSDK.getBitmapInscriptionId(
    177700
  )
```

Get the sat containing the canonical Bitmap inscription:

```js
const sat =
  await BitmapSDK.getBitmapSat(
    177700
  )
```

Bitmap SDK does not simply trust an inscription because its text looks correct.

Canonical Bitmap validation checks the protocol rules and resolves the correct claim.

For interactive maps that cannot afford an unbounded post-OCI scan in one user action,
use the bounded resumable scanner:

```js
const scan =
  await BitmapSDK.scanBitmapClaim(
    950000,
    {
      maxScanBlocks: 64
    }
  )

if (scan.state === 'found') {
  console.log(scan.inscriptionId)
}

if (
  scan.state === 'missing'
  && scan.complete === false
) {
  console.log(
    'Scanned through',
    scan.scannedThroughHeight,
    'resume later from SDK progress'
  )
}
```

The SDK controls the resume point from trusted scan progress. It keeps progress
in memory even when IndexedDB is unavailable, and persists it when storage is
available. Each saved checkpoint is tied to its block hash; if that hash changes,
the SDK discards the cursor and restarts from the Bitmap height. A caller cannot
skip unscanned heights and then treat a later claim as canonical.


---

# Shared Bitmap block index (v1.0.3)

Bitmap SDK now records **per-block Bitcoin coverage** in a separate IndexedDB
store named `bitmapBlockIndex`. Each record saves the height, validated block
hash and minimal valid raw-text Bitmap claim candidates. It **also records empty
blocks**, so an earlier scan can accelerate a different Bitmap lookup.

The SDK does **not** equate an indexed candidate with a canonical winner.
Lookups still proceed in ascending Bitcoin height order, verify candidate
inscriptions against their genesis-block raw bodies, reject invalid and
premature claims, and prefer the earliest valid claim. The shared index is
local browser acceleration, not a central authority.

### Opt-in bounded background range indexing

```js
const summary = await BitmapSDK.indexBitmapBlocks({
  // Omit startHeight to resume the previously indexed modern range.
  // Use an explicit height to backfill historical Bitcoin blocks.
  maxBlocks: 256,     // cap each invocation, 1..2048
  concurrency: 8,     // parallel block requests, 1..16
  confirmations: 6,   // skip the most recent 6 blocks
  onProgress: p => {
    console.log(p.height, p.processedBlocks, p.fetchedBlocks);
  }
});

console.log(summary);
// { startHeight, scannedThroughHeight, processedBlocks,
//   fetchedBlocks, reusedBlocks, bitmapCandidates, nextHeight, ... }
```

Call this function repeatedly when building the browser's complete Bitmap
coverage; it deliberately limits work per invocation. By default it starts
at height **942,000**, where the SDK's modern live discovery begins. Earlier
Bitmaps still use the existing historical OCI fallback; to build local coverage
before the cutover, supply `startHeight` explicitly.

The indexing cursor is saved in IndexedDB when available. Subsequent calls
revisit a small recent overlap to check for reorganisations, while ensuring
forward progress even with small `maxBlocks` settings. If IndexedDB is
unavailable, the in-memory cache still helps during the current page session,
but the cursor is not persisted.

`scanBitmapClaim()` and `getBitmapInscriptionId()` automatically reuse
this index. The existing `watchBlocks()` also adds scanned blocks to the
shared coverage store, allowing newly mined blocks to participate in future
lookups without another raw-block download.

### Trust, reorganisation and performance notes

- Before reusing a stored block, the SDK confirms its stored block hash
  against `/r/blockhash/<height>`. If it changes, affected local canonical
  caches and search checkpoints are rewound before fetching current data.
- New raw-block indexing checks the height's hash both before and after parsing
  to avoid persisting an index across a detected reorganisation.
- Scans run concurrently in 16-height batches, but **candidate selection is
  processed in ascending height order**, even when requests finish out of order.
- A previously covered block ordinarily needs a lightweight hash check rather
  than another `/r/block/<height>` raw download. This lowers raw-block bytes
  and parsing effort but does **not** eliminate blockchain requests.
- IndexedDB belongs to the browser origin. It is **not a shared index between
  unrelated browsers or inscription origins**, and it is not an on-chain
  snapshot.
- This is a source-level optimisation; live full-chain performance and
  persistent IndexedDB/reorganisation behaviour need real-browser validation.
  Do not treat an incomplete local range as evidence a Bitmap is unclaimed.

The v1.0.3 database upgrade adds the block store (schema version 3) while
preserving the validation protocol version, so compatible existing cache
records are not deliberately discarded.


---

# Parcels

Validate a Parcel:

```js
const parcel =
  await BitmapSDK.validateParcel(
    2,
    177700
  )
```

Get the canonical Parcel inscription:

```js
const parcelId =
  await BitmapSDK.getParcelInscriptionId(
    2,
    177700
  )
```

Get all canonical Parcels belonging to a Bitmap:

```js
const parcels =
  await BitmapSDK.getBitmapParcels(
    177700
  )
```

Get higher-level Parcel data:

```js
const parcel =
  await BitmapSDK.getParcelData(
    2,
    177700
  )
```

Validate a particular inscription as a Parcel claim:

```js
const result =
  await BitmapSDK.validateParcelInscription(
    'INSCRIPTION_ID'
  )
```

Do not assume that text such as:

```text
2.177700.bitmap
```

is automatically a valid or canonical Parcel.

Bitmap SDK handles the Parcel protocol rules, provenance checks, transaction bounds and canonical tie-break.

---

## Parcel tie-breaks

When multiple valid claims compete for the same Parcel, Bitmap SDK applies the protocol tie-break:

1. Earlier genesis height wins.
2. If both claims have the same height, inscription IDs are compared using numeric-aware ordering.

Applications should use the SDK result rather than implementing a separate tie-break.

---


## Compact Mondrian pattern manifests (experimental v1.0.3)

Compact patterns can make Bitmap geometry portable without carrying an entire
raw Bitcoin block. For block `840000`, an independently exported pattern with
3,050 elements occupies **1,525 bytes** when nibble-packed. The user's browser
gzip test produced **479 bytes**; offline Brotli quality 11 achieved **405
bytes** for the exact same 1,525 bytes. Actual inscription payload and runtime
size depend on the index representation and decoder.

Use these **opt-in** public APIs:

```js
// Export an integrity-checked candidate from live recursive Bitcoin data.
// "auto" tries built-in Brotli, then gzip, otherwise leaves the data unpacked.
const manifest = await BitmapSDK.getBitmapPatternManifest(840000, {
  forceLive: true,
  compression: "auto" // or "none", "gzip", "brotli"
})

// Verify SHA-256, unpack size classes and inspect the derived pattern.
// This does NOT establish independent on-chain authenticity.
const decoded = await BitmapSDK.decodeBitmapPatternManifest(manifest)
console.log(decoded.pattern.length, decoded.integrityVerified)

// Geometry reconstruction does not fetch /r/block unless requested.
const preview = await BitmapSDK.getBitmapGeometryFromManifest(manifest, {
  includeEmptySpaces: true
})
console.log(preview.slots.length, preview.emptySpaces.length)
console.log(preview.verifiedAgainstRawBlock) // false

// Explicit canonical verification before allowing a new manifest into
// the SDK's trusted, local IndexedDB pattern cache:
await BitmapSDK.cacheBitmapPatternManifest(manifest)

// Subsequent calls can reuse raw-verified compact pattern data.
// Changed block hashes cause this cache to be skipped.
const info = await BitmapSDK.getBitmapPatternInfo(840000)
console.log(info.source)
```

For directly supplied size arrays, `packBitmapPattern(pattern)`,
`unpackBitmapPattern(bytes, transactionCount)`, and
`getBitmapGeometryFromPattern(height, pattern, {includeEmptySpaces:true})`
are also available.

**Brotli compatibility:** The SDK first probes native
`DecompressionStream("brotli")` (or `CompressionStream("brotli")` for encoding).
That particular JavaScript stream format differs from the browser's
automatic decoding of `Content-Encoding: br` HTTP responses and is not
available in every browser.

Where the native stream format is unavailable, the SDK automatically
reuses the already-inscribed Nexus BrotliOC module:
`/content/fb483c53bc6938c1df1e25e03e99f726d08a1c0d2db4e0e644c0baf3526a1d60i0`.
It exposes `globalThis.BrotliOC.compress(bytes, {quality:11})` and
`globalThis.BrotliOC.decompress(bytes)`, and is lazy-loaded only when needed.
If the host HTML has already loaded the classic script
`/content/b1d16a7a1ada08b5c7f51837478f578c0abd0973809c439228f28ccd5c38e44ai0`
and exposes `globalThis.BrotliDecode(bytes)`, the SDK can also reuse that
decoder without another import. Both paths stay on the existing Ord
`/content/` recursive origin. A separate 5ea902d20bbe... inscription URL
was supplied but its runtime API has not yet been verified, so no dependency
on that URL is introduced.

An explicit `decodeBitmapPatternManifest(manifest, {brotliDecoder})`
callback remains supported and takes priority over automatic selection.
The module's compressed result is validated against the uncompressed
pattern length, nibble range and SHA-256 before being accepted. No third-party
CDN or separately bundled Brotli implementation is required.

**Note:** Brotli-compressed *inscription content* using the Ord tag-9
`content-encoding: br` mechanism is distinct from a Base64 Brotli
field inside a JSON manifest. The latter requires the manifest decoding path
described above; do not add a second Brotli layer accidentally.

**Security and trust:** The manifest's SHA-256 authenticates nothing on its
own if the publisher controls both the payload and checksum. A matching
Bitcoin block hash and transaction count likewise do not prove that the
nibble pattern was derived correctly. The preview API explicitly marks
imported geometry `verifiedAgainstRawBlock: false` and **never inserts it
into the trusted geometry cache**. The trusted cache import first recomputes
the entire pattern from `/r/block/<height>` and checks hash stability.
Cache entries store the raw-verified compressed representation separately
from existing full Mondrian layouts and re-check the block hash on reads.
These protections are deliberate; simply supplying `verifyAgainstRawBlock:
false` cannot bypass trusted import.

There is **no automatic fetch from an unverified external manifest URL** and
no newly inscribed on-chain index. The read-path first prefers the existing
full Mondrian geometry record, then the independently verified local compact
pattern store before falling back to the existing on-chain Mondrian pattern
module and raw Bitcoin block. Exact transaction output values still require
the raw block. This is an experimental SDK branch, not an inscribed release.

# Mondrian geometry

Get the deterministic Bitmap layout:

```js
const mondrian =
  await BitmapSDK.getBitmapMondrian(
    177700
  )
```

Include current Parcel claims:

```js
const mondrian =
  await BitmapSDK.getBitmapMondrian(
    177700,
    {
      includeClaims: true
    }
  )
```

Request exact transaction output totals only when an application needs them:

```js
const mondrianWithValues =
  await BitmapSDK.getBitmapMondrian(
    177700,
    {
      includeValues: true
    }
  )

console.log(
  mondrianWithValues.slots[0].valueSats
)
```

`includeValues` intentionally uses the recursive raw block path because
the compact pattern index stores size classes rather than exact transaction
values. Exact values are not persisted into the shared Mondrian cache.

Useful slot data includes:

```js
slot.parcelNumber
slot.x
slot.y
slot.size
slot.valueSats
slot.claimed
slot.inscriptionId
```

The underlying Bitmap transaction-size pattern can also be requested directly:

```js
const pattern =
  await BitmapSDK.getBitmapPattern(
    177700
  )
```

Inspect how the pattern was resolved:

```js
const patternInfo =
  await BitmapSDK.getBitmapPatternInfo(
    177700
  )

console.log(patternInfo.source)
console.log(patternInfo.indexed)
console.log(patternInfo.cached)
```

`indexed === true` means the pattern came from the canonical on-chain
Mondrian/Bitmon pattern index. `cached` only describes whether IndexedDB
accelerated the lookup; it does not change the underlying provenance.

Bitmap SDK can derive live Mondrian data from the recursive raw block endpoint:

```text
/r/block/<height>
```

so applications do not need a non-recursive `/block/...` request.

---

# Provenance

Build a parent/child provenance graph from an inscription:

```js
const graph =
  await BitmapSDK.getChildrenTree(
    inscriptionId,
    {
      maxDepth: 3,
      maxNodes: 1000
    }
  )
```

Use:

```js
graph.nodes
graph.edges
```

The SDK represents provenance as a graph because Ordinal inscriptions may have more than one parent.

Do not assume that every inscription relationship is a simple one-parent tree.

---

# Bitmap tree

Get a combined Bitmap view:

```js
const tree =
  await BitmapSDK.getBitmapTree(
    177700,
    {
      maxDepth: 3,
      maxNodes: 1000,
      includeReinscriptions: 'root'
    }
  )
```

Useful fields include:

```js
tree.bitmap
tree.parcels
tree.provenance.nodes
tree.provenance.edges
tree.reinscriptions
```

---

# Reinscriptions

Get inscriptions sharing the same sat:

```js
const chain =
  await BitmapSDK.getReinscriptionChain(
    inscriptionId
  )
```

Parent/child provenance and same-sat reinscriptions are different relationships and should not be merged together.

Some Ord servers may not expose the required sat index.

Handle this gracefully:

```js
if (
  chain.supported === false
) {
  console.log(
    'Sat index unavailable'
  )
}
```

---

# Shared local Bitmap storage

Bitmap SDK includes a standard IndexedDB database:

```text
Database:
bitmap-sdk
```

Current validation/storage protocol:

```text
bitmap-sdk-v1.0.1
```

The goal is to avoid every Bitmap application independently rebuilding the same local index.

A compatible app can store validated:

- Bitmaps
- Parcels
- Bitmap state
- Mondrian layouts
- Parcel claims
- Child/provenance trees
- Same-sat chains
- Block hashes
- Bitmap search progress
- Watcher progress

Check storage availability:

```js
await BitmapSDK.storage.ready()

const status =
  await BitmapSDK.storage.getStatus()

console.log(status)
```

Read stored Bitmap information:

```js
const bitmap =
  await BitmapSDK.storage.getBitmap(
    177700
  )
```

Read stored Parcels:

```js
const parcels =
  await BitmapSDK.storage.getBitmapParcels(
    177700
  )
```

Subscribe to SDK storage updates:

```js
const unsubscribe =
  BitmapSDK.storage.subscribe(
    event => {
      console.log(event)
    }
  )

// Later:
unsubscribe()
```

---

## Why use the Bitmap SDK storage standard?

Without a common storage layer, multiple Bitmap applications may independently:

- Resolve the same Bitmap
- Load the same children
- Revalidate the same Parcels
- Rebuild the same Mondrian geometry
- Rebuild the same provenance trees
- Recheck the same block data

Bitmap SDK provides a common structure for that information.

When multiple compatible applications run within the **same browser origin and storage context**, they can potentially reuse the same `bitmap-sdk` IndexedDB data instead of starting again from zero.

This can make Bitmap applications progressively faster as the local index becomes populated.

### Important limitation

IndexedDB is browser-origin/storage-context scoped.

It is **not** a global Bitcoin index and it is not automatically shared between:

- Different domains
- Different browsers
- Different browser profiles
- Private/incognito sessions
- Sandboxed contexts with isolated or unavailable storage

Ord preview iframes may also run under sandbox restrictions that affect storage availability or sharing.

Bitmap SDK therefore treats IndexedDB as an optimisation only.

If storage is unavailable, the SDK falls back to memory and live Bitcoin/Ord recursive data.

---

## Validation versioning

Cached validation results are tagged with the SDK storage protocol version.

For example:

```text
bitmap-sdk-v1.0.1
```

If validation logic changes in a later release, old validation records must not silently remain authoritative.

Bitmap SDK invalidates incompatible cached validation data instead of trusting results generated under different rules.

This is important because the cache must never become a second source of truth.

Bitcoin and Ord remain the source of truth.

---

# Synchronising a Bitmap

Refresh and store application-ready Bitmap data:

```js
const data =
  await BitmapSDK.syncBitmap(
    177700
  )
```

This is useful when an application wants to deliberately populate or refresh its local SDK index.

---

# Block scanning

Scan one Bitcoin block:

```js
const result =
  await BitmapSDK.scanBlock(
    blockHeight
  )
```

The default scanner is deliberately designed to avoid exploding into hundreds of content requests on inscription-heavy blocks.

Encoded or delegated inscriptions can be deferred during the fast scan.

The result can indicate that the scan was not exhaustive:

```js
result.unresolvedEncodedOrDelegatedCount
result.exhaustiveParcelDiscovery
```

---

## Exhaustive block scan

If an application genuinely needs to resolve encoded/delegated Parcel candidates immediately:

```js
const result =
  await BitmapSDK.scanBlock(
    blockHeight,
    {
      resolveEncodedParcelCandidates: true
    }
  )
```

This can be significantly more expensive and should not normally be required for routine block watching.

Canonical Parcel APIs still perform the full validation required when a Bitmap or Parcel is requested directly.

---

# Latest block

Scan the current Bitcoin tip:

```js
const latest =
  await BitmapSDK.getLatestBlockUpdates()
```

---

# Block watcher

Watch sequential Bitcoin blocks:

```js
const watcher =
  BitmapSDK.watchBlocks(
    event => {

      console.log(
        event.newBitmaps
      )

      console.log(
        event.newParcels
      )

    },
    {
      pollIntervalMs: 30000,

      scanCurrent: true,

      persist: true,

      resumeFromStorage: true,

      resolveEncodedParcelCandidates: false
    }
  )

await watcher.ready
```

Stop it later:

```js
watcher.stop()
```

The watcher can persist its progress in IndexedDB so a compatible application can continue rather than unnecessarily beginning again.

---

# Fast watcher vs exhaustive discovery

The default:

```js
resolveEncodedParcelCandidates: false
```

is intended for efficient continuous watching.

This prevents a single inscription-heavy Bitcoin block from triggering hundreds of content requests.

Use:

```js
resolveEncodedParcelCandidates: true
```

only when exhaustive encoded/delegated Parcel discovery during the block scan is specifically required.

---

# Reorg handling

The block watcher stores recent block hashes and can detect when a previously processed height no longer matches the current chain.

On a detected reorganisation, SDK state can be rewound and rescanned from an earlier safe height.

---

